- Story & Context: By the autumn of the mall’s total demolition, local runaways gathered around the drained ceramic fountain of St. Jude’s Atrium, treating the gutted shell of a Galaga cabinet like a roadside altar. Mira had left her ruined Rhodes permanently wired to the building's lingering emergency circuit, so whenever municipal power surges washed through the dead grid, the piano struck low, self-generating chords that resonated through the empty concourse. The wrecking ball crew refused to strike the west wing for three weeks because the air smelled of ozone and the arcade speakers kept weeping without human hands touching the keys. A pirate radio station broadcast the automated death-song across the valley, transmitting Mira’s looping farewell into the car stereos of the suburbs: "The lights go out, but the quarters never fall."
